---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, DEFAULT_QUOTA, SITE_TITLE, WHAT_YOU_GET, X_HANDLE, X_URL } from "../consts";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`Pricing — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Everything on nano-api is free while the service is young. No card, no trial clock, no feature held back."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="page">
				<p class="eyebrow">Pricing</p>
				<h1>Free, for now. All of it.</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					Both services, every feature, no card and no trial clock. Ask for a key and you get one
					the same day.
				</p>

				<div class="card offer">
					<p class="price">$0</p>
					<p class="quota">
						{DEFAULT_QUOTA} monitors or schedules to start — ask and it goes up, still free
					</p>
					<ul>
						{WHAT_YOU_GET.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
					</ul>
					<a class="button" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=nano-api%20key`}>Get an API key</a>
				</div>

				<h2>Why free</h2>
				<p>
					This is run by one person alongside a day job. Charging money makes promises about
					response times, uptime and longevity that I am not ready to make yet — and I would rather
					have a handful of people actually using it, telling me what breaks, than a pricing page
					that converts nobody. The infrastructure costs a few dollars a month whether you use it
					or not, so your monitor is not what makes this expensive.
				</p>

				<h2>What happens when that changes</h2>
				<p>
					Paid plans will arrive when the service has earned them, probably some months out. When
					they do:
				</p>
				<ul class="promises">
					<li>You get plenty of notice, by email, before anything costs money.</li>
					<li>Nothing gets switched off without you agreeing to it first.</li>
					<li>
						Nothing you built breaks. The API does not change shape because the billing does.
					</li>
					<li>
						Anyone using it before then keeps a free allowance. Early users are the reason it got
						good.
					</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Limits that exist anyway</h2>
				<p>
					These are technical, not commercial — they keep one account from taking the service down
					for everyone:
				</p>
				<ul class="promises">
					<li>
						{DEFAULT_QUOTA} monitors or schedules by default. Mail me and it goes up; there is no
						charge and no negotiation.
					</li>
					<li>Ping payloads up to 2 KB. Relay request bodies up to 8 KB.</li>
					<li>Scheduled calls: timeout up to 60 seconds, up to 3 attempts per run.</li>
					<li>Schedules may only call public HTTPS endpoints.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Questions you would reasonably ask</h2>

				<div class="qa">
					<h3>How do I get a key?</h3>
					<p>
						Mail <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or ping
						<a href={X_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{X_HANDLE}</a> on X. Provisioning is
						manual on purpose: a human reads every request, and there is no signup form to trust.
					</p>

					<h3>What happens when I hit the limit?</h3>
					<p>
						Creating the next monitor or schedule is refused with a clear error. Pings to monitors
						that already exist, and runs of schedules that already exist, are
						<strong>never</strong> refused for quota reasons — a limit must not be able to switch
						off your monitoring quietly.
					</p>

					<h3>How fast will I know something is wrong?</h3>
					<p>
						A sweep runs every minute, so an alert lands within about a minute of your interval
						plus grace period passing. If you need second-level precision, say so — that is a
						different product, and I would rather tell you than sell you this one.
					</p>

					<h3>Where do alerts go?</h3>
					<p>
						To any HTTPS webhook. The body is Slack-shaped (<code>text</code> at the top level), so
						a Slack incoming webhook works with no configuration, and anything else gets structured
						JSON in the same request. Email and SMS alerts are not built.
					</p>

					<h3>Is there an API for everything?</h3>
					<p>
						There is only an API — monitors, schedules, history, and your own keys. No dashboard to
						learn, on purpose. The <a href="/howto/">how-to page</a> is the whole product in curl.
					</p>

					<h3>What if the service itself goes down?</h3>
					<p>
						Then you are not monitored, and honesty is the only answer available: there is no SLA
						while it is free, and I will not promise one I cannot keep afterwards. It runs on
						Cloudflare's edge with a serverless database, which is a good bet, not a guarantee. For
						monitoring where a missed alert has serious consequences, run two independent
						providers.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="closing card">
					<h2>Start now, pay nothing</h2>
					<p>Both services, {DEFAULT_QUOTA} things watched or run, upgradeable by asking.</p>
					<a class="button" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=nano-api%20key`}>Get an API key</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.page {
		max-width: 46em;
		padding: 2em 0 0;
	}
	.lede {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 1.05rem;
	}
	.offer {
		margin: 2em 0 1em;
		border-color: var(--alive-dim);
		box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--alive-dim), var(--box-shadow);
	}
	.price {
		font-size: 3rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		margin: 0;
		letter-spacing: -0.03em;
	}
	.quota {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		color: var(--alive);
		margin: 0.2em 0 1.2em;
	}
	.offer ul {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0 0 1.6em;
		padding: 1.2em 0 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
		gap: 0.5em 1.4em;
		font-size: 0.93rem;
	}
	.offer li {
		position: relative;
		padding-left: 1.5em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.offer li::before {
		content: "✓";
		position: absolute;
		left: 0;
		color: var(--alive);
	}
	h2 {
		margin-top: 2.4em;
		font-size: 1.5rem;
	}
	.page p {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	ul.promises {
		padding-left: 1.2em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	ul.promises li {
		margin-bottom: 0.5em;
	}
	.qa h3 {
		margin-top: 1.8em;
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--text);
	}
	.closing {
		margin-top: 3em;
		text-align: center;
	}
	.closing h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}
	.closing p {
		margin-bottom: 1.4em;
	}
	@media (max-width: 620px) {
		.offer ul {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>
